distressing scenes in copenhagen  fans and players watched on in horror as  denmark star  christian eriksen collapsed in their  euro 2020 match against finland  his teammates formed a human shield  around him as he was given cpr on the  pitch for several minutes before being  taken away on a stretcher  the fixture was suspended with those  left in the stadium fearing the worst  but then more positive news a photograph  emerged showing the midfielder raising  his hand as he was carried off  it was then confirmed by the tournament  organizers that eriksen was stable and  conscious in hospital  denmark's football association said  their players were able to communicate  directly with ericsson  and the decision was taken by both teams  to restart the game  the finns clapping the danes back onto  the field  before going on to win one nil  [Applause]  i'm very affected myself i was involved  in a similar situation as a coach where  we almost lost someone  everyone is now reaching out to their  family trying to facetime them  all of our thoughts and prayers are with  christian and his family  ericsson was clearly on the minds of the  teams playing in the later game in group  e  romelu lukaku scored the opening goal in  belgium's 3-0 win over russia  he's a teammate of eritsen at inter  milan and dedicated the goal to him  shocking scenes but this has happened  before in 2012 fabrice muamba collapsed  on the field while playing for his club  in the fa cup in england  muamba said erickson's collapse brought  him to tears  it's almost like it broke back stuff  that i've  put down in me i've this emotion that  just  deep down there i never want to relieve  it again and then to  watch it from that distance and not  knowing  the expectation was gonna happen  it's scary  ericsson is 29 years old and has never  contracted covert 19  and has no medical conditions that he's  aware of according to inter's team  physician  he has passed every medical exam since  joining from tottenham 18 months ago  erickson remains in hospital and is  undergoing tests  peter stem at al jazeera well sanjay  sharma is a professor of cardiology at  st  george's university of london he also  worked with premier league clubs  including tottenham  where eriksen once played and joins me  now on skype  uh sanjay we've seen this sort of thing  happen before with fabrice muamba who we  heard from there and tragically with  mark vivian foway  what causes these sudden collapses  i'll start i'd like to start by saying  that they are uncommon they affect one  in fifty thousand athletes  but when they do occur they're usually  due to an underlying heart fault usually  a genetic fault that runs in families  or a congenital fault that someone's  born with and these faults may affect  the muscle of the heart  the electrical system of the heart or  even the blood supply of the heart  so you know as you as uh the commentator  just said that  uh people that are high level sports  people  are tested for these types of conditions  because of the impact that a cardiac  arrest has  not just on them and their families but  also on the general population  and christian ericsson certainly was  tested for  several years whilst he was a player at  spurs and then went on to have a normal  medical assessment  at inter milan and what this begs the  question as to what happened  you know was it something that these  cardiac screening programs  were unable to identify such as a subtle  scar in the heart  or a blocked artery or it was it  something that either quiet maybe he'd  had a viral illness  or was slightly below par before he  played i'm sure that these  answers may come to light after the  danish cardiologists have finished all  of their investigations  the medical teams there in copenhagen  reacted incredibly fast last night  how crucial is the time to the survival  of someone who's had an episode like  this  and how important is it to have  equipment like a defibrillator on site  well we must congratulate them i think  the the efforts were fantastic and as a  result of that we've got a  young man who is alive and will will  live for several decades  um it's absolutely crucial to get the  heart started once there's a sudden  cardiac arrest because for every minute  that lapses the chances of survival drop  by between  seven and ten percent so when someone  has a cardiac arrest the important thing  is to start  cpr immediately within two minutes which  was the case  to keep all the other organs perfused  such as the brain clearly if the brain  doesn't get  oxygen for enough time then the  individual could be left with brain  damage in the future  and of course whilst keeping that uh  resuscitation process going if there is  a shockable rhythm such as ventricular  fibrillation which was the case with  fabrice muamba  the important aim is to deliver an  electrical shock within two or three  minutes to try and get that heart  started as quickly as possible and it  seems  that this is what happened in that  christian eriksen's downtime  that is the time between the collapse  and the time that  they got everything going again was  really very short and therefore his  outlook is good  it's actually crucial that these  defibrillators pitch side defibrillators  are available  i can only speak for the english  football association where all  92 clubs affiliated with the english  football association do have  such pitch side care professor sanjay  sharma thank you very much for joining  us and your insight on that  thank you
 

